 ('S01', 'Part 1', 'ill', 'sh4_1', 'What kills you is not the black hole itself, but the difference in pull across your body.'),
 ('S02', 'Part 2', 'ill', 'sh4_2', 'The size of the black hole makes all the difference between peaceful wonder and instant destruction. Fall toward a stellar-mass black hole, an object only a few times heavier than our Sun, and your body is destroyed before you even reach the boundary.'),
 ('S03', 'Part 3', 'ill', 'sh4_3', 'Why does mass matter so much? The answer lies in tidal forces, the very same physics that creates ocean tides on our planet.'),
 ('S04', 'Part 4', 'ill', 'sh4_4', 'Gravity weakens with distance, which means that any object falling feet-first feels a stronger gravitational pull at its feet than at its head. On Earth, this difference across the height of a human body is so microscopic that our senses cannot detect it.'),
 ('S05', 'Part 5', 'ill', 'sh4_5', 'But near a small, stellar-mass black hole, the event horizon is tiny, perhaps only thirty kilometers across. Because the center is so extraordinarily close to the surface, the steepness of the gravitational slope across just two meters of space becomes catastrophic.'),
 ('S06', 'Part 6', 'ill', 'sh4_6', "If you fell into a small black hole, the difference between the pull on your boots and the pull on your helmet would be millions of times Earth's gravity. At the exact same time, the gravitational field lines would converge inward toward the center, squeezing your shoulders tightly together."),
 ('S07', 'Part 7', 'ill', 'sh4_7', 'Physicists have a wonderfully descriptive, almost humorous term for this horrifying process: spaghettification. Your body would be stretched into a thin, fragile thread of matter, pulled apart cell by cell, long before your boots touched the horizon.'),
 ('S08', 'Part 8', 'ill', 'sh4_8', 'Now swap in a supermassive black hole. At its horizon the ride is gentle. But as you draw near the central singularity, the gravitational field lines converge with absolute brutality.'),
 ('S09', 'Part 9', 'ill', 'sh4_9', 'The tidal forces that were harmless at the horizon grow rapidly stronger with every millimeter you fall.'),
 ('S10', 'Part 10', 'ill', 'sh4_10', 'First, you feel a faint, persistent tension stretching your spine, as if someone were pulling your boots while holding your shoulders. Within fractions of a second, that gentle tug turns into an irresistible, tearing force.'),
 ('S11', 'Part 11', 'ill', 'sh4_11', 'The difference in gravitational acceleration between the top of your head and the soles of your feet spikes into thousands of gravities, then millions. Your spacesuit is shredded, the bonds between your molecules are pulled apart, and finally, the very atoms of your body are stretched into long, thin ribbons of matter.'),
